: "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:46:31.640Z", "request_id": "req_011CgAkSfd38bvvkK4ZRqsyw", "thinking_duration_ms": 91416}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgAkSg3b5Ugmc6C1LGDf8", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'd pick **candidate 4**. This is a rough mental estimate, not a full simulation.\n\n**1. Batch plans 3 and 7 lose to daily ordering.**\n- Ordering every 2–3 days saves 24 for each day without an order. In exchange, roughly an extra day of demand (about 56 units) sits on the shelf, at 0.7 per unit per day. That costs about 39 per cycle against a saving of 24–48, so batching comes out behind.\n- Candidate 3 has an extra problem: on day 24, 217 units arrive (65 + 152) on top of the leftover stock. Anything above the 228 capacity is lost after it has been paid for.\n\n**2. Among the daily plans, the difference comes from product 1.**\n- The daily plans, apart from 3 and 7, order each day's forecast 3 days ahead, scaled up or down by a different factor per plan.\n- Product 1 keeps for 8 days. To spoil, a unit would need about 230 units of stock in front of it, which no plan reaches. So the real costs are lost sales, holding, and leftovers at the end, which are worthless.\n- One unit of product 1 is worth ordering if it sells with probability above about 0.39. Selling it gains 28 (price 20 plus the avoided penalty of 8); not selling it loses the 11 purchase price.\n- Forecast demand for days 3–29 totals 915; with the multiplier's mean of 1.046 that's about 957 expected, give or take about 60.\n\nApproximate cost for product 1, as lost sales × 28 + total ordered × 11 + holding:\n\n| Candidate | Product 1 order level | Total ordered | Estimated cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 | exactly the forecast | 915 | ≈ 12 500 (about 70 lost sales) |\n| 4 | +5 %, plus +27 on day 0 | 983 | ≈ 12 300 (about 25 lost sales) |\n| 1 | ×1.1, plus +15 on day 0 | ≈ 1017 | ≈ 12 540 |\n| 2 | ×1.15 | ≈ 1052 | worse than 4 |\n| 0 | ×1.2 | ≈ 1098 | worse than 4 |\n| 6 | ×1.3 | ≈ 1190 | worse than 4 |\n\n- For candidate 1, the units beyond about 1000 sell with probability of only about 0.3, below the 0.39 threshold.\n- For candidates 0, 2 and 6, the large surplus mostly ends up as worthless leftover stock at the end, plus holding costs.\n\n**3. Product 0 hardly separates the plans.**\n- An extra unit gains 5 if sold but loses 11 if not, so ordering exactly the forecast or a little less is close to optimal.\n- Candidates 0, 4 and 5 all order exactly the forecast for product 0. Candidate 2's 0.9× gives about the same result. Candidates 1 and 6 order a little too much.\n\n**4. Robustness.** Candidate 4 orders slightly above expected demand and adds a buffer at the start, so it holds up best when demand deviates from the forecast in either direction."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5807, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 355}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:46:41.837Z", "request_id": "req_011CgAkSfd38bvvkK4ZRqsyw"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "inde